data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration needed.** Your code uses only `Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, and `Thread.Sleep` — these APIs work identically on .NET 8. It will compile and run correctly as-is.

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort): You could simplify with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.Run`, but that's a design preference, not a migration requirement.

⚠️ **One real bug to fix:** Your `foreach` captures the loop variable `item` by reference. In .NET Framework with older C# versions, all threads may process the *last* item. Use a local copy:

```csharp
foreach (var item in items)
{
    var localItem = item;
    var t = new Thread(() => Process(localItem));
    ...
}
```

(In C# 5+, `foreach` variables are scoped per iteration, so this is safe if you're on a modern compiler — but worth verifying.)
